{-
   A reference has a size whatever it names, and an erased body may hold one.

   Compiled with specialization declined (the `.generic` marker beside this), because that is the
   only mode in which any of it was ever wrong: specialized, `%a` becomes `%I32` before lowering sees
   it and every question below has an ordinary answer.

   Two defects, both older than the fixture and both found the same way - by `lib/Native`'s `isNull`
   ceasing to be an intrinsic and becoming one line of Yana, which put a body of exactly this shape
   into every program.

   **A pointer had no size when its pointee was a variable.** `ReprTable::compute` returned early for
   any type mentioning one, so `sizeOf(%a)` was zero: `memoryWidth` asserted on a load of no bytes,
   and `genTypeDesc` asked the schema for a descriptor slot that no schema records - because a body
   needing a descriptor for a pointer is a body needing something it never asked its caller for. The
   two questions had been sharing one answer. `Type::generic` means *mentions a variable*, which is
   what substitution needs; `opaque` means *this layout is the caller's to know*, which for a
   reference is true of the pointee and false of the reference.

   **And an erased witness thunk boxed one.** `witness.cpp` asked `isGeneric` where the result
   position beside it already asked `isMemoryType`, so a thunk declared its `%a` parameter as a
   pointer *to* one while the call site passed it directly - and the implementation, compiled against
   `%a`, read the address of the address. `lower_call.cpp` had been corrected and this had not, which
   is exactly the kind of disagreement one question asked in two places produces.
-}

import Native

-- The shape that reaches both. Nothing here is about pointers being special: it is a generic body
-- that reinterprets one, which is what `isNull` is.
fn addressValue(p: %a) -> Size = bitcast(p) :: Size

-- The library's own two, written out so that this fixture states what it is protecting rather than
-- pointing at another file. `null` binds `a` from the *result* and from nothing else, which is the
-- case with no argument to take a descriptor from.
fn nowhere() -> %a = bitcast(0 :: Size)
fn absent(it: %a) -> Bool = addressValue(it) == 0

-- A borrow travels the same road and is the other half of the repr rule: `'a` is one word however
-- large `a` is, and `type.def` puts `Ptr` and `Borrow` on `kTypeDirect` in the same breath. Taking
-- its address is what turns the second kind into the first inside a body that can see neither.
fn borrowedAddress(x: 'a) -> Size = addressValue(addressOf(x))

data Cell {v: Int}

fn main() -> Int:
    let &cell = Cell {v: 7}
    let p = addressOf(cell)

    -- A real address is not zero, and the null one is - through the generic bodies above rather
    -- than through the intrinsics they replaced.
    if absent(p) then return 1
    if !absent(nowhere() :: %Cell) then return 2

    if borrowedAddress(cell) == 0 then return 3
    if addressValue(p) == 0 then return 4

    return 0
